(4295069, 615113, 6999, '<td valign="top">If we have to have more examples of the effect of slow shutter speed on moving water, then this is at least the way to do it - much more interesting then all the other efforts.</td>', 'If we have to have more examples of the effect of slow shutter speed on moving water, then this is at least the way to do it - much more interesting then all the other efforts.', '2007-12-06 06:40:18.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295074, 614456, 6999, '<td valign="top">Very cool photo; you''ve given us a bare minimum of information really, which is ideal, and have resisted the temptation to make the left half of frame completely black, which shows strong compositional sense. Possibly my winner in this challenge.</td>', 'Very cool photo; you''ve given us a bare minimum of information really, which is ideal, and have resisted the temptation to make the left half of frame completely black, which shows strong compositional sense. Possibly my winner in this challenge.', '2007-12-06 06:42:47.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295076, 616316, 6999, '<td valign="top">I like your eye for a composition, and your sense of colour too. The organic shapes of the creeping plant - as well as the disorder of the boxes in the window add a sense of the random to the more obviously regular subject and presentation. Fine work.</td>', 'I like your eye for a composition, and your sense of colour too. The organic shapes of the creeping plant - as well as the disorder of the boxes in the window add a sense of the random to the more obviously regular subject and presentation. Fine work.', '2007-12-06 06:46:16.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295078, 617904, 6999, '<td valign="top">Very strong image; I like that you''ve caught a sense of resilient loneliness here; a sense of holding out against the tides of trash and what I guess she''d consider the ''uncouth''. Strong photograph.</td>', 'Very strong image; I like that you''ve caught a sense of resilient loneliness here; a sense of holding out against the tides of trash and what I guess she''d consider the ''uncouth''. Strong photograph.', '2007-12-06 06:48:01.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295091, 618384, 80058, '<td valign="top">The lighting here is excellent, nice and soft but pose wise I think this is a bit too flat... I find that when using a more traditional approach to portraits placing the model front on is not too flattering. Non the less very nice expression from the 2 models ;)</td>', 'The lighting here is excellent, nice and soft but pose wise I think this is a bit too flat... I find that when using a more traditional approach to portraits placing the model front on is not too flattering. Non the less very nice expression from the 2 models ;)', '2007-12-06 06:54:43.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295095, 617002, 6999, '<td valign="top">Nice crop - ''fine art'' indeed! Two dynamics strike my eye - the first and most obvious is the window reflection, which, despite your title, brings to my mind a sense of the impossibility of the permanent - or at the very least a presentiment of that impossibility: the two figures, evidently still bound in the affection, but broken up by the strange geometry of the architecture. A sense of hope there, but the very sense of hope requires an admission that one cannot be certain of any future whatsoever.<br/> <br/>Secondly, there are the two almost ghostly, almost angelic figures further up the street: in the pose you have caught strongly suggesting a more aged couple - yet still with contact, still with affection as it seems. In some senses presenting an ideal of what might possibly lie ahead, a sense of all we could reasonably hope for; two directions, the possibility of fracture, of the interference of the hard geometry of the world, and yet also the evident possibility that those might be overcome.<br/> <br/>To top that off with your cheeky suggestion of some greater relevance to it all in that cropping of that sign; the more I look the more I think its simply a masterpiece. Thankyou.</td>', 'Nice crop - ''fine art'' indeed! Two dynamics strike my eye - the first and most obvious is the window reflection, which, despite your title, brings to my mind a sense of the impossibility of the permanent - or at the very least a presentiment of that impossibility: the two figures, evidently still bound in the affection, but broken up by the strange geometry of the architecture. A sense of hope there, but the very sense of hope requires an admission that one cannot be certain of any future whatsoever. Secondly, there are the two almost ghostly, almost angelic figures further up the street: in the pose you have caught strongly suggesting a more aged couple - yet still with contact, still with affection as it seems. In some senses presenting an ideal of what might possibly lie ahead, a sense of all we could reasonably hope for; two directions, the possibility of fracture, of the interference of the hard geometry of the world, and yet also the evident possibility that those might be overcome. To top that off with your cheeky suggestion of some greater relevance to it all in that cropping of that sign; the more I look the more I think its simply a masterpiece. Thankyou.', '2007-12-06 06:56:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(4295097, 618529, 80058, '<td valign="top">oooooooh this is interesting, to be hones tho from the thumbnail I didn''t even thought of clicking on it...glad I decided to now... I think its a great scene captured, composition is lovely! It''s a great silhouette altho I''m not sure how you got some of the sea to turn black like that, thats the only lil fault for me but I enjoyed this image</td>', 'oooooooh this is interesting, to be hones tho from the thumbnail I didn''t even thought of clicking on it...glad I decided to now... I think its a great scene captured, composition is lovely! It''s a great silhouette altho I''m not sure how you got some of the sea to turn black like that, thats the only lil fault for me but I enjoyed this image', '2007-12-06 06:57:25.000000', NULL, 1),
